KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ explicitly instantiated: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ extern template: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
no body for callee
--
N5008 [temp.explicit]/12: `extern template int box<int>::put(short);'
suppresses only the implicit instantiation; the program's explicit
instantiation definition defines the same specialization.  The parser
keeps such a declaration of a member function, which becomes a bodiless
member hiding the template ("no body for callee box<int>::put").
Discarding it, as for a free function, fixes this, but with libstdc++
<fstream> it then dropped basic_ofstream's move constructor ("member
basic_ios<char>::set_rdbuf ... not found",
cpp11_mem_initializer_names_virtual_base_through_member_typedef timed
out), likely the shape of
cpp11_class_instantiated_while_its_base_is_instantiated.  g++ 13 and
clang++ 18 accept this program, and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
